# Feature Audit: AgentMesh and Trust

## 1. Scope

This audit covers AgentMesh handshakes, trust cards, trust scoring, mesh messages/handoffs, revocation/quarantine interactions, duplicated TypeScript services, and SDK support. It inspects `product_platform/trust`, `product_platform/mesh`, `packages/agentmesh`, `services/api`, and SDK package surfaces. Relevant benchmarks are Microsoft Agent 365 identity/quarantine, Temporal Nexus service contracts, Google Agent Gateway, AWS AgentCore Identity, and Agent Governance Toolkit runtime policy enforcement. Out of scope: base auth/API key behavior, covered in AUTH.

## 2. Current Implementation Summary

Product Platform has trust models and repositories in `packages/product-platform/src/product_platform/trust`, mesh models and persistence in `product_platform/mesh`, and UI pages for mesh/trust. Lower-level Python AgentMesh code includes handshake, credentials, trust cards, marketplace fallback, and CLI utilities under `ophanix-python-sdk/packages/agentmesh`. A separate TypeScript `services/api` tree contains in-memory registry/trust/handshake routes. Product mesh APIs persist messages and handoffs, including caller-supplied decision/result fields.

Current workflow: agents and mesh entities can be represented, trust cards can be issued/verified in a demo-key mode, messages and handoffs can be recorded, and trust score pipelines can update records. The codebase has multiple trust ontologies and handshake paths, and policy enforcement is not consistently server-side.

